<Project>

  <!--
    Settings every project in the authorization server inherits.

    Two of these are load-bearing rather than stylistic, and removing them
    silently weakens the security posture rather than breaking the build:

      TreatWarningsAsErrors  - the nullable-reference warnings are how a
                               "this can never be null" claim in a protocol
                               path stays true. Downgrading them to warnings
                               means the next person's null check is optional.

      EnforceCodeStyleInBuild - the analyzer rules in .editorconfig include the
                               ones that ban the APIs a redirect-URI comparison
                               must never touch (see N-03 in the requirements:
                               System.Uri may never be the comparison input).
                               Those bans are worthless if they are suggestions.
  -->

  <!--
    The version every package in this repository publishes at.

    It is a single literal on purpose. Every packable project here is built from one tree and
    released together, so a per-project version would be one literal per package to forget instead
    of one in total. No number is written here: a library packs unless told otherwise, so a count
    read off the files has been wrong twice. `StructuralRuleTests.PackableProjects` is the approved
    set, checked by a test rather than maintained as a sentence here.

    The 0.x line is the honest claim for a server whose own README lists what is not built yet;
    1.0.0 would say more than this gets to say.

    **This said 0.1.0 "because nothing here has ever been published", and that was false when it was
    written.** Every id in this repository was already on nuget.org at 0.1.0 — one
    `curl https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/boltway.mcp/index.json` settles it, and eighteen of
    them answered. Publishing from that state would have packed 0.1.0 against a feed that already
    held 0.1.0, and the paragraph below describes exactly what happens next. The comment was the
    only thing saying the feed was empty, and a comment is a claim; the feed is the fact. Check it
    before trusting a sentence here, this one included.

    The rule that outlives this number: **once a version is published, it is frozen.** A feed will
    not take a second 0.1.0 and will not tell anybody it refused — it skips the duplicate and the
    workflow stays green, so the new assembly simply never ships. That failure is silent on the
    publishing side and loud on the consuming side, in the worst possible place: a downstream
    consumer restores the frozen package, compiles green (the C# compiler does not check a call
    that lives inside an already-compiled dependency), and throws MissingMethodException at
    startup. That is not hypothetical — it took a production container into a restart loop behind
    a 502 until it was rolled back.

    Two consequences worth stating plainly:

      - Bump this number in the same commit as the public change, not at release time.
      - A ProjectReference packs as a dependency on the referenced project's version, so a project
        whose own source did not change still needs a bump when something it references moves.
        Otherwise the frozen nuspec names a dependency that is merely old, and a consumer pinning
        it can never receive the newer one no matter how often that one is published.

    `scripts/check-published-versions.py` runs in the publish workflow and fails when a packed
    version is already in the feed with different contents. That guard is what makes forgetting
    cost twenty minutes instead of a rollback.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>0.2.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>

    <!-- Reproducible output: the same source must produce the same bytes,
         because "is the deployed binary the reviewed source" needs an answer. -->
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>

    <!-- An authorization server has no business calling unmanaged code. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

    <!--
      Culture *identity* without culture *data*, and the split is the point.

      `InvariantGlobalization` implies `PredefinedCulturesOnly`, so `new CultureInfo("vi")` throws:
      only the invariant culture exists. That is fatal for the interaction pages' localization, which
      needs a BCP 47 tag as a dictionary key, as the value of `<html lang>`, and as something
      `RequestLocalizationMiddleware` can match `ui_locales` against.

      What it does NOT need is ICU. Nothing on those pages formats a number, a date or a currency,
      and no comparison anywhere in this repository is culture-sensitive — the ban list forbids the
      normalizing members of System.Uri, every string comparison is Ordinal or OrdinalIgnoreCase by
      rule, and `LOWER()`-style folding is done in C# on a normalized column rather than by a
      collation. So allowing a culture to be *named* costs nothing that the invariant setting was
      protecting, and the unmanaged-code reason above stands unchanged.

      A culture created this way carries invariant data. That is correct here and would not be for an
      application that formatted anything: this one puts the tag in an attribute and looks a
      dictionary up with it.
    -->
    <PredefinedCulturesOnly>false</PredefinedCulturesOnly>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Package metadata">
    <Authors>Tobii (Truong To)</Authors>
    <Product>Boltway</Product>
    <Copyright>Copyright © 2026 Tobii (Truong To)</Copyright>

    <!--
      An SPDX expression rather than the deprecated PackageLicenseUrl, so a consumer's licence
      scanner reads it off the nuspec instead of following a link that may not resolve in five
      years. Apache-2.0 rather than MIT for the patent grant: this is protocol code, and a
      contributor licensing their patents alongside their copyright is the difference that matters
      to anyone adopting it inside a company.
    -->
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/TobiiNT/Boltway</PackageProjectUrl>
    <!--
      No `dpop`. It was in this list while `spec/REQUIREMENTS.md` S-37 said DPoP is deferred and
      "advertise nothing DPoP-related", and §4 of the same file says advertising it "invites proofs
      we reject". A search tag is an advertisement: somebody filtering nuget.org for `dpop` was
      being shown a package that does not implement it. That is N-06 — the rule this repository
      cites more than any other — on the one surface a stranger meets before the README.

      Put it back in the commit that implements RFC 9449, not before.
    -->
    <PackageTags>oauth;oauth2;oidc;authorization-server;mcp;pkce;rfc9728;aspnetcore</PackageTags>

    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/TobiiNT/Boltway</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>

    <!--
      A symbol package beside every library. A stack trace from a consumer's production incident is
      worth more than the few hundred kilobytes, and it is the one artefact that cannot be produced
      after the fact.
    -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    A package cannot ship without saying what it is.

    Fifteen of eighteen reached nuget.org carrying MSBuild's literal default, `Package Description`,
    because nothing anywhere required otherwise: `Description` is the one field that has to be
    per-project, so the shared block above could not supply it and no check noticed its absence.
    `Description` is also the only thing a person reads before choosing between sixteen
    similarly-named `Boltway.*` packages, so the failure was invisible internally and total
    externally.

    It fires at pack time, which is where the nuspec is written — so it is only a gate if something
    packs before a tag is cut. CI does now; that is the other half of this fix and it is in
    `.github/workflows/ci.yml`.
  -->
  <!--
    Nothing detected a breaking change. No PublicApiAnalyzers, no ApiCompat, no baseline — a removed
    or re-signatured public member produced a green build and a published package, and
    `Boltway.PublicApi.Tests` is a compile canary over a handful of seams rather than a surface
    baseline.

    This is the SDK's own ApiCompat: it downloads the baseline package and diffs real signatures,
    so it catches the `int`-where-a-`long`-was case that `scripts/check-published-versions.py`
    documents itself as unable to see (it compares metadata *names*, and a signature can change
    without a name changing).

    The baseline is the release before this one, because that is what a consumer has already
    restored. At 0.x a break is permitted — `VERSIONING.md` says so — and the point is not to forbid
    it but to make it a thing somebody decided rather than a thing nobody noticed.

    **It moves in the same commit as `<Version>`, and leaving it behind is silent in one direction.**
    A baseline a release too old still catches everything that release inherited, so most of the gate
    keeps working and nothing looks wrong. What it stops seeing is a member the previous release
    *added* and this one removes: the baseline package never carried it, so there is nothing to
    report — and that is exactly the member a consumer on the newest release compiles against.
    Measured on 2026-08-24 against a 0.1.0 baseline while 0.2.0 was the released version: making
    `AuthorizationServerMetadata.ScopesSupported` internal failed the pack with two CP0002 errors,
    and making `PromptValuesSupported` internal — same file, same kind of break, added in 0.2.0 —
    packed green. Pointing the baseline at 0.2.0 turned the second one red.

    Never equal to `<Version>`: a baseline equal to the version being packed validates nothing, also
    measured, so the move is *after* the version rather than alongside it.
    `StructuralRuleTests.The_api_compatibility_baseline_is_the_previous_released_version` holds this
    to CHANGELOG.md's second heading, so forgetting costs a red test rather than a quiet release.

    Pack-time and network-bound, so it does not slow an ordinary `dotnet build` and does not make an
    offline one fail. The failure looks like `CP0002: Member ... exists on [Baseline] ... but not
    on ...`, verified by making one public property internal and watching it fire.

    **Its own error message suggests the wrong fix.** It offers
    `/p:ApiCompatGenerateSuppressionFile=true`, which writes the break into an XML file nobody
    reads again — the shape CLAUDE.md rules out for the architecture tests, for the same reason. A
    deliberate break at 0.x is recorded by moving the version and saying so in CHANGELOG.md, where
    the consumer who is about to hit it will see it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false'">
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
    <PackageValidationBaselineVersion>0.1.0</PackageValidationBaselineVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="RefuseDefaultPackageDescription"
          BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec"
          Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false' AND ('$(Description)' == '' OR '$(Description)' == 'Package Description')">
    <Error Text="$(PackageId) has no &lt;Description&gt;, so it would publish with the literal string 'Package Description' on its nuget.org listing. Say what the package is, and what it does not drag in." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    The readme every package carries. One file at the root rather than a per-project one: a reader
    who lands on any of the packages should reach the same explanation of what the whole thing is,
    and one near-identical file per package would drift within a release.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false'">
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
